        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Gas Water Heater Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size by first-hour rating, not tank gallons: pick a heater whose first-hour rating meets or beats the hot water your household uses in its busiest hour. For most families of four that means a first-hour rating of about 75 to 90 gallons, usually from a 50-gallon tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why first-hour rating beats tank gallons</h3>

          <p>
            Tank size only tells you how much hot water sits in storage. The first-hour rating, listed on the EnergyGuide label and in the specifications, tells you how much hot water the heater can supply over one hour starting from a full tank, counting the stored water plus whatever the burner reheats in that hour. Gas burners recover quickly, so depending on burner size a 40-gallon gas tank often posts a first-hour rating in the 60s or 70s, and a 50-gallon one in the 80s or 90s.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you work out your peak hour?</h3>

          <p>
            Picture your busiest hour, which for most homes is the morning, with showers, a dishwasher and a sink all running. A typical shower draws roughly 10 to 20 gallons of hot water depending on its length and flow rate, a dishwasher about 6, and a warm clothes wash somewhere around 7 to 15. Total what happens in that hour, then choose a heater whose first-hour rating equals or exceeds it.
          </p>

