          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you match the old heater?</h3>

          <p>
            Only if it kept up. A household that kept running out of hot water should size up; one that has shrunk may be able to size down and save on both the purchase and standby loss. Give a soaking tub extra thought, since a large tub alone can drain most of a 40-gallon tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will the new tank fit?</h3>

          <p>
            Measure before you order. A 50-gallon tank is normally taller or wider than a 40, and a 75 is far bigger than either. Check the height to the ceiling or any obstruction, the floor area, and every doorway and stair on the route in. The installation manual lists the clearances needed for combustion air and service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Venting Type Does Your Home Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whatever the existing vent supports, in most cases: an atmospheric tank drops into a working chimney or B-vent, while a home without one needs a power-vent, direct-vent or condensing model. The four types differ in power needs, vent pipe and price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Atmospheric vent</h3>

          <p>
            These heaters let hot exhaust rise on its own up a chimney or metal B-vent. Standard models need no electricity, cost the least and are what most older homes already have. The catch is that they need a correctly sized, clear vertical vent and enough combustion air in the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power vent</h3>

          <p>
            A blower drives the exhaust through plastic pipe, so the heater can vent sideways through a wall. That suits homes with no usable chimney, or homes where a furnace upgrade has left the old shared chimney wrong for the water heater alone. Power-vent units need a 120-volt outlet and cannot heat water without power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Direct vent</h3>

          <p>
            Direct-vent heaters pull combustion air from outside and send exhaust back outside through a concentric or two-pipe vent, keeping combustion sealed off from the living space. Many run without electricity. They earn their place in tight, well-sealed homes and small utility closets where room air is scarce.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensing</h3>

          <p>
            Condensing heaters draw out so much heat that the exhaust is cool enough for PVC pipe, and they produce condensate that has to be drained. They are the most efficient gas tanks and the most expensive. Replacing an atmospheric unit with one means adding power, a drain and new venting.
          </p>

