        <div id="uef" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Does UEF Mean on a Gas Water Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            UEF, or Uniform Energy Factor, is the share of the gas energy that reaches the tap as hot water over a standard day of use, with standby losses from the tank sitting idle included. Higher is better, and the jump from a standard tank to a condensing one is large.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What UEF is typical?</h3>

          <p>
            Standard atmospheric gas tanks usually land in the low 0.6s. Higher-efficiency non-condensing models reach the high 0.6s to 0.7s, and condensing models sit above 0.8.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the draw pattern matters</h3>

          <p>
            Each heater's UEF is rated within a draw pattern, very small, low, medium or high, set by its first-hour rating. Putting a medium-draw model's UEF next to a high-draw model's tells you less than comparing two heaters in the same pattern. The EnergyGuide label also shows an estimated yearly energy cost at national average prices, which makes a handy side-by-side for two specific models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is higher efficiency worth paying for?</h3>

          <p>
            It depends on how much hot water you use and what your gas costs. A family of four that goes through a lot of hot water gains far more from a condensing heater than someone living alone. Take the yearly cost estimate from each label, adjust it for your local gas price, and divide the yearly difference into the extra installed cost to get a rough payback period.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Energy Star models qualify for rebates?</h3>

          <p>
            Sometimes. Energy Star certified gas tanks have to clear a higher efficiency bar than the federal minimum, and that certification can open up utility rebates in some areas, so call your gas company before you buy. Programs change often, and some need pre-approval or a participating contractor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is a Gas Tank Better Than Tankless, Electric or a Heat Pump?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a like-for-like swap where gas and a working vent are already in place, yes: a gas tank is the simplest and cheapest choice. The more you would have to change, venting, gas line or electrical work, the more the alternatives earn a look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a gas tank wins and loses</h3>

          <p>
            Gas tanks pair a low purchase price with fast recovery and an easy install when gas and venting already exist. Against that, they lose heat on standby, typically last roughly 8 to 12 years, and bring the combustion safety concerns that come with any gas appliance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas tankless</h3>

          <p>
            Tankless gas heaters supply hot water without a break and last longer, often 15 to 20 years. They cost much more to install, usually need a bigger gas line and new venting, and can have trouble feeding several fixtures at once where incoming water is cold. They suit long ownership and households that would rather have endless hot water than a low upfront price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric resistance and heat pump</h3>

          <p>
            Electric resistance tanks are inexpensive and easy to install, but they recover slowly and usually cost more to run than gas where gas prices are moderate. Heat pump water heaters are the most efficient electric choice and use a fraction of the electricity a resistance tank needs. If you are thinking of leaving gas, our guide to the <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">best heat pump water heaters</a> covers space, noise and payback.
          </p>

